---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { LIMITS } from "../lib/api";
import { QUICKNET } from "../lib/drand";

const maxYears = LIMITS.maxUnlockSeconds / (365 * 86400);
const toc = [
	["prove", "Can I prove I knew something early?"],
	["encrypted-when", "Can you tell when a message was encrypted?"],
	["who", "Does it prove who wrote it?"],
	["unlock-time", "Can I see the unlock time without decrypting?"],
	["notes", "What are the lines above the encrypted block?"],
	["other-tools", "Will other tools open it?"],
	["early", "Can anyone open it early?"],
	["lost", "What if I lose the encrypted text?"],
	["drand-stops", "What if drand stops?"],
	["see-message", "Can you see my message?"],
];
---

<Base
	title="FAQ · timelock.dev"
	description="Short answers about timelock.dev: proving you knew something early, unlock times, the plain notes above the encrypted block, other tools, and what can go wrong."
>
	<div class="container layout">
		<article class="layout-main markdown">
			<header class="page-head">
				<h1>FAQ</h1>
				<p class="lead">Short answers. For the details, see <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a> and its <a href="/how-it-works#limitations">limitations</a>.</p>
			</header>

			<h2 id="prove">Can I prove I knew something early?</h2>
			<p>Yes, with one extra step. The encrypted text can't show when it was made, so a public timestamp has to do that part:</p>
			<ol>
				<li>Encrypt your message here, with an unlock time after the event.</li>
				<li>Before the unlock time, post the encrypted text somewhere public that records the date, such as a social post, a git commit or a gist.</li>
				<li>After the unlock time, anyone can paste it into this site and read it.</li>
			</ol>
			<p>The public timestamp proves <em>when</em> you had it. The unlock proves <em>what</em> it said. Nobody, including you, can read or change the message in between.</p>

			<h2 id="encrypted-when">Can you tell when a message was encrypted?</h2>
			<p>
				No. The encrypted text contains only the drand round it unlocks at and the drand chain hash. There is no "encrypted at" time, before or
				after decryption. Our <a href="/#activity">activity list</a> records when something was encrypted, but it can't be linked to any message.
				To prove when you wrote something, use a public timestamp as <a href="#prove">described above</a>.
			</p>

			<h2 id="who">Does it prove who wrote it?</h2>
			<p>No. Anyone can encrypt anything to any round. Posting from your own account, or signing the post, is what ties it to you.</p>

			<h2 id="unlock-time">Can I see the unlock time without decrypting?</h2>
			<p>
				Yes. The round is in the header of the encrypted text, and anyone can read it. Paste it into <a href="/#decrypt">Decrypt</a>, and the
				site shows the unlock time right away. You can't hide it.
			</p>

			<h2 id="notes">What are the lines above the encrypted block?</h2>
			<p>Optional plain notes. When you encrypt, two checkboxes add them, and both are on by default:</p>
			<pre><code>Unlock at https://timelock.dev
Unlocks: Sat, Nov 21, 2026 9:08 AM EST (2026-11-21 14:08:27 UTC)

-----BEGIN AGE ENCRYPTED FILE-----
…</code></pre>
			<p>
				They tell a reader where to go and when. <strong>They are not encrypted or protected.</strong> Anyone can edit or delete them, so trust the
				round inside the block, not the note. When you paste, this site reads the round from the block and warns you if a note says a different
				time.
			</p>

			<h2 id="other-tools">Will other tools open it?</h2>
			<p>
				The block, yes. <code>dee</code>, <code>tle</code> and other tlock tools open it, and this site opens theirs. But
				<strong>tools other than this site need just the BEGIN…END block.</strong> We tested <code>dee</code>, Go <code>tle</code> and
				<code>age</code>, Rust <code>tlock_age</code> and <code>tlock-js</code>, and all of them reject any text before or after the block. Use
				<strong>Copy block only</strong>, turn the notes off, or strip them:
			</p>
			<pre><code>sed -n '/^-----BEGIN AGE/,/^-----END AGE/p' message.txt | dee crypt --decrypt</code></pre>
			<p>This site and the <a href="/docs#decrypt">API</a> accept the text with or without notes.</p>

			<h2 id="early">Can anyone open it early?</h2>
			<p>
				No. Not you, not us. The key for a round doesn't exist until drand publishes it. The only way around that is for most
				<a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/leagueofentropy/">League of Entropy</a> members to cheat together.
			</p>

			<h2 id="lost">What if I lose the encrypted text?</h2>
			<p>The message is gone. We don't keep a copy, and there is no recovery.</p>

			<h2 id="drand-stops">What if drand stops?</h2>
			<p>
				The message stays locked until drand quicknet publishes your round. If drand shut down for good, nobody could open it. Locks of up to
				{maxYears} years are allowed, but be careful with long ones. A new round arrives every {QUICKNET.period} seconds.
			</p>

			<h2 id="see-message">Can you see my message?</h2>
			<p>
				On the website, no: it encrypts in your browser. The <a href="/docs">API</a> encrypts on our server, so it sees your message in memory. We
				don't store or log it. For anything sensitive, <a href="/how-it-works#offline">encrypt offline</a>.
			</p>
		</article>

		<aside class="layout-side sticky toc-side" aria-label="On this page">
			<nav class="Box">
				<div class="Box-header compact"><h2 class="Box-title">On this page</h2></div>
				<ul class="toc">
					{toc.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
				</ul>
			</nav>
		</aside>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	@media (max-width: 1011px) {
		.toc-side {
			display: none;
		}
	}
</style>
